      "Capital Gains and Losses",
      "If you sell something for more than you paid, the profit is a capital gain. Capital gains can come from stocks, options, bonds, mutual funds, or real estate.\n\nThe holding period decides the tax:\n• Held 1 year or less: a short-term gain, taxed at the ordinary income tax rate.\n• Held more than 1 year: a long-term gain, taxed at a lower rate of 0%, 15%, or 20%, depending on income.\n\nThe holding period clock starts the day after the purchase and ends on the day of the sale. Trade dates are used, not settlement dates.\n\nIf losses are bigger than gains, the net capital loss can reduce ordinary income. But only $3,000 of net loss can be deducted in any 1 year. Any extra loss carries forward to next year.\n\nExample: A customer has $5,000 of gains and $20,000 of losses. The net loss is $15,000. She deducts $3,000 this year, and $12,000 carries forward.\n\nGains are not taxed until they are realized. A stock that goes up but is not sold has an unrealized gain, sometimes called a paper profit. The tax on an unrealized gain is 0%. The gain is realized when a long position is sold or a short position is covered (bought back).",
