      "Finding the Cost Basis",
      "Cost basis is what you paid for a security plus any commissions. Net sales proceeds are what you got when you sold, minus any commissions. Gain or loss equals net proceeds minus cost basis.\n\nExample: An investor buys 100 shares at $20 and pays a $40 commission, so the cost basis is $2,040. She sells at $25 and pays another $40, so net proceeds are $2,460. Her capital gain is $420.\n\nWhen shares were bought at different times and prices, there are ways to choose which ones were sold:\n• Share identification: the customer tells the broker, by the settlement date, which shares she is selling. She might sell the shares that cost the most to get a smaller gain, or even a loss.\n• First-in, first-out (FIFO): the default. If the customer does not choose, the IRS uses the cost of the first shares bought. Since prices tend to rise over time, FIFO usually gives the biggest gain.\n• Average cost: used for mutual fund shares, along with the other two.",
