      "Taxes on Dividends",
      "Dividends can be qualified or nonqualified. Qualified dividends are taxed at the investor's capital gains rate. Nonqualified dividends are taxed at the ordinary income rate.\n\nCash dividends from corporate stock, both common and preferred, are usually taxed at 15%. For people in the highest bracket, the rate is 20%. These lower rates do not apply to interest from corporate bonds, which is taxed as ordinary income.\n\nMutual funds pass qualified dividends on to shareholders. The fund shows which dividends are qualified on Form 1099-DIV.\n\nPayers report portfolio income to the IRS each year and send copies to customers:\n• Form 1099-DIV shows cash dividends, dividends that qualify for lower rates, and capital gains distributions from investment companies and REITs.\n• Form 1099-INT shows bond interest, split into taxable and nontaxable (municipal) interest.",
